ming about a virus mutating  we get mutations and viruses all the  time coronavirus in fact less than  others  if i were being unkind to our health  secretary i would accuse him  of having launched a new project fear i  mean at the time that the health  secretary  was talking about us all being rather  more careful as we edged towards  christmas  it does seem that this the emergence of  this new virus is being used  to make us think a lot more about the  way in which we behave i don't think  that's that's fair i'm not going to  trade  scientific qualifications with someone  as learning as that um the point is that  the chief medical officer the chief  scientific advisor  scientists at porting down are looking  at this strain there is a concern that  has contributed  to the fast spread of the infection in  recent weeks but we're investigating it  and hopefully  it will turn out to be less of a concern  than feared but the key issue  is there is light at the end of the  tunnel we have the vaccine eight hundred  thousand now  uh already of the visa vaccine uh in the  uk we're deploying that  at pace and i think for many of your  viewers that is the light  at the end of sunday that's why we need  people to continue to adhere  to the social distancing restrictions  because there is light at the end of the  tunnel  can we talk about about christmas then  in your mind  will is there any possibility that this  government will look again  about the christmas window given the  rate of infection  is where it is given that you know in  eight days time you know after eight  days of you know tier three  conditions here in london you know  hundreds of thousands of people will be  traveling to various parts of the  country  to see their loved ones and potentially  taking covert 19 with them  you can't tell me that this doesn't  remain under review but all things are  always kept under review but there's the  balance the christmas could be cancelled  is it no there's a balance to be struck  many families have not seen each other  all year  it is important for people's well-being  for their mental health we don't want to  criminalize people for coming together  sure as family over christmas  but people shouldn't misinterpret what  the guidelines say  it's not saying that people must go and  see family  it's saying that where families want to  see each other they won't be  criminalized  for doing so but it's important people  do the minimum  that is is possible so people will make  their own judgments as families will  make  their own judgments i won't be seeing my  parents uh over christmas so people  same here their own judgments on these  but we don't want to criminalize  families for coming together  and we must recognize it's been a  difficult year and many families will  want to see each other over the  christmas break i understand all of that  but it is confusing to me this morning  given that in you know 24 hours we will  be in tier 3 here in london  that the advice from government for  people in tier 3 the highest  tier let's not forget is make your own  minds up about this i mean would your  advice  to me be to travel home to scotland this  house  has always been to to the british public  to exercise their common sense which  which they have done and we've had  debates in the past as to whether every  single  aspect of guidance has been prescribed  and we've tried to  to treat the british public as grown-ups  and ensure that people deploy their  common sense what we're saying with  christmas  is people should do the minimum that is  possible but they will make their own  judgments  as families and we don't want to  criminalize families for coming together  over the christmas period but people  should exercise  the minimum that they're able to and  that's what the chief medical officer  set out  in the press conference yesterday and i  think that's what the vast majority of  families will do they'll respond  responsibly  and they will d